#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3a246c-800f-5999-9b93-b9ed1cd1d295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296022} {:answer/id #uuid "dd461320-244c-5daa-985d-40605a216206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7c0266-869b-5f46-9251-f7d45ad77979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296024} {:answer/id #uuid "db6d40c5-e2c0-5946-99d2-7d8f1a508394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296025} {:answer/id #uuid "f5319bce-f88c-581b-a6dd-645e7a59d343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd461320-244c-5daa-985d-40605a216206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a0b78-80b6-526e-91f3-053687642870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffd06503-0d35-5908-aec5-906d9832f7e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120971" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x-2)(y+1)^{2}.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>-1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/a66a57932dbc935b5ed6f74b4f0faa0047c4b7ef58ded46be088dad30fa5eee4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/52efe97fcffa3da05151b3ffef5305e0c3ec0d567d3f62b964a30740c08a3981.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/aa100568c18b31507c775e77ed3201a5aed6f708b541e48046617a43fe9a8304.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0642538-6b85-5e67-9f42-396e0fb3dd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177251} {:answer/id #uuid "df09ae57-bd34-542c-a436-1c558fbc4407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177252} {:answer/id #uuid "10ee1709-b0de-5ae3-886f-fd6c092f567e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177253} {:answer/id #uuid "43cd8039-c23b-5ddb-879a-f4abc6b5b3f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177254} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9856c0-486f-5b7f-8994-065089dccd36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ee1709-b0de-5ae3-886f-fd6c092f567e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121090-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177253} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb8638e-fe07-56fe-bff6-9e8706b5887f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42cb2492-ecd7-59e3-9c43-d91c9543c6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-121090" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04199958-5d5e-5ba5-80e2-da514c357032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/957d13976822d3424932c2dfda994546f8b5b8f9f0bdd6443726f01f0e08d7d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a97f9a-1673-5ccd-83e5-2921c2b688f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/d7d8592ce28b5e57aa1a8c883fe750f63dc6d50a1ce5dba00cdcaadc7ea7f5f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350395} {:answer/id #uuid "e5286a77-c9d6-5a3a-b0a2-93ddf4b6d2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/5588d90960db9c441d970ffb9ce68a88c2c207806684af4c74e3b296d8919635.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350396} {:answer/id #uuid "6942cbcc-16da-58a2-880c-ace38fa3b13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/bc65521c39db9c5eeaac8aa7aa3fb824286e6ed701f3d7d2ded9f2fdabb4d8d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350397} {:answer/id #uuid "c884b4e9-f527-582d-be6d-56943d1f1f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/068f75ffaabb14f9c68941066a408d31fc7579ab3881e2d8e4b545044b3cd8a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04199958-5d5e-5ba5-80e2-da514c357032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/957d13976822d3424932c2dfda994546f8b5b8f9f0bdd6443726f01f0e08d7d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350394} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dedb5b8-d3ec-56e0-bc9c-2c529411ece3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dafc3f03-586d-571d-89f8-3616dc177366" :question/math-academy-id "q-121272" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-2){y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/3222e808ccb652a4bfb3b3291099b87f5a1db5d4d38635d58c6649d8efc71f06.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/e8ab67f737067fb0e0bfc64a8060fdefacf79ba430bdf9a24d21dbc7893b1cf7.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/42e3960fe067a02d6263df15ede7224ecd6b977c744139889d83529d873096bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b727039b-016b-5dcd-a554-589bc1eb5dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f0547f4bf2530cbcc271e81ba501c3710b0359bb222d1e56014acac6675b515d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330297} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb86582-4a82-5cc1-a06f-708fd5a7166c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc93