 inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/e4d289aef282e076b062aab6d8f0f209396313e2c6403b2e27d1c0272ac90795.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/4cef3532a8e70098c030f2341800f06e9e54e4074126a0a97aecdf1f1c5185d0.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/c9ed172c5746b1b0afd2d9b7b3bbb21245115df164bfb7e34f4f530887376e28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7422b24-5612-5acb-ad4a-265e834503a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350401} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc42baf-aff5-5693-8c51-9dea1d7d42c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fc42baf-aff5-5693-8c51-9dea1d7d42c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350402} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf269d6-0165-587b-bd6e-aed9f3ad