12b1-5c07-5fd8-a519-108d0f0b250c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97a088c4-2639-5910-a368-297d780dde49" :question/math-academy-id "q-326445" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{3}(y-1).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/7a10e8b18faa71b030ff09bff5ea847bdab9d1ad8b6bf52738ee670a22ad9657.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/b0cb6dff5c7425a9b1f86779e50b85e70c9d98b1a78f1000d17fc9ccd32daea8.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^{3}$ | $(y-1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,0)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,0)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14065421/assets/7e42173ebe7cbf85a1bca0bb8cff1bc95841e309b7cc737f84435c2a45485aa7.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |"}]}
